INT. VICTORIAN SEANCE PARLOR - NIGHT

Velvet drapes, a round table, a crystal ball veiled in black gauze. A single candle gutters. Rain taps the windows.

MADAME ODILE FONTAINE-VANCE, 50s, turban and shawl, sits with her palms flat on the table, eyes closed. Across from her, ALISTAIR DRAKE, 40s, widower, grips the edge of his chair. And in the corner, her assistant, a nervous girl of twenty, PRUDENCE MILLWARD, tends a hidden gramophone.

                    ODILE
Spirits of the beyond. We call upon you.

The candle flickers. A low moan sounds from beyond the drapes.

                    ALISTAIR
(whispering)
Is it her?

                    ODILE
Hush.

The moan rises. The table shudders. Alistair's knuckles whiten.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(hollow, distant)
Alistair...

He gasps. Half rises.

                    ALISTAIR
Margaret?

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(hollow, distant)
I am here, my love.

Prudence, in the corner, goes pale. She mouths at Odile, shaking her head. Not me. Odile's eyes snap open. She stares at the gramophone, which is silent.

The voice comes again, deeper, nearer.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(hollow, distant)
Tell the fraud to stop.

Odile's hands fly from the table. Prudence knocks over her stool.

                    ALISTAIR
(slowly)
That's not funny, Madame.

                    ODILE
It is not me.

The candle blows out. Darkness. Somewhere a window bangs open, and the drapes snap like sails.
